From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:46:45 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: share an exported texture through host memory

A Venus context can only import a resource the render server can
receive: a dma-buf, or a shared-memory segment. An Apple host has
neither GBM nor dma-buf, and a vrend texture's storage is a GL object
with no fd at all, so proxy_context_attach_resource() reported
"exported res N to unexpected fd_type -1" for every gralloc buffer an
Android guest handed to its Vulkan driver, and vkr answered every
vkGetMemoryResourcePropertiesMESA with "invalid res_id".

The renderer already shares memory the other way round: a Venus
context exports a blob as a segment and vrend_resource_import_shm()
maps it and refreshes a texture from it before each read. Give the
export direction the same shape. When a resource with no fd of its own
is asked to export one -- which happens only when another context is
importing it -- allocate a host segment the size of the texture's
linear layout, snapshot the texture into it, and hand out its fd as
VIRGL_RESOURCE_FD_SHM. The segment becomes the resource's shared
storage, recorded in the same shm_import_* fields an imported blob
uses, so a single refresh path serves both directions and both pin the
segment through the same helper. The proxy then carries such an export
to the render server, which already imports a segment; only its
dma-buf-only check stood in the way, and the size it takes from the
exported descriptor now falls back to the size the file reports,
because a shared-memory object need not be seekable and on this host
is not.

A row of the segment is the texture's width rounded up to the first
whole number of pixels that makes it a multiple of 16 bytes, the pitch
Metal reads a texture over a buffer at. The guest allocator pads the
buffer it asks for by the same rule, and it is the layout the
importing driver is told about; the texture itself stays as wide as
the guest samples it.

Either side may hold the newer pixels, and the resource records which:

  - GL to importer. Every GL write to the resource marks it dirty: a
    draw or clear into it as an attachment, a blit, copy or transfer
    into it, a shader image store. Binding it as a colour attachment
    is not enough, because a frame can span many command buffers that
    never bind the framebuffer again. Before the renderer creates its
    next fence it writes every dirty resource back to its segment; a
    pinned segment is written by the GPU, queued ahead of the fence,
    which then covers the copy. The guest fences its work before it
    passes a buffer on, so that fence is the earliest point another
    consumer can look at the pixels. Handing the fd to another context
    writes the segment at once.
  - Importer to GL. While the resource is clean, the texture is
    refreshed from the segment before every GL read of it -- through a
    sampler view, or as the source of a blit, copy or readback -- and
    before the first GL write that keeps any of its pixels. While it is
    dirty, GL holds the newer pixels and a read leaves the texture
    alone; a refresh would overwrite them with the segment's older
    copy.

Neither direction adds a host-side fence: ordering comes from the
guest, exactly as it does for an imported blob. A buffer that is never
exported keeps ordinary texture storage and an unchanged code path,
and the whole export conversion is Metal-host only, so a host with
dma-buf keeps its GBM export untouched.
---
 src/proxy/proxy_context.c  |  23 ++-
 src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c | 317 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 src/vrend/vrend_renderer.h |   8 +
 3 files changed, 316 insertions(+), 32 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/proxy/proxy_context.c b/src/proxy/proxy_context.c
index 4f87d5accabf1929a5b174d4a5f95419aa699e61..64f318d741670840cddc388cb88d257723323687 100644
--- a/src/proxy/proxy_context.c
+++ b/src/proxy/proxy_context.c
@@ -471,9 +471,11 @@ proxy_context_attach_resource(struct virgl_context *base, struct virgl_resource
    uint64_t res_size = res->map_size;
    bool close_res_fd = false;
    if (res_fd_type == VIRGL_RESOURCE_FD_INVALID) {
-      /* importable pipe resouce can only export as dma-buf */
+      /* An importable pipe resource exports as a dma-buf, or, on a host with
+       * no dma-buf, as the shared-memory segment the renderer gave it. */
       res_fd_type = virgl_resource_export_fd(res, &res_fd);
-      if (res_fd_type != VIRGL_RESOURCE_FD_DMABUF) {
+      if (res_fd_type != VIRGL_RESOURCE_FD_DMABUF &&
+          res_fd_type != VIRGL_RESOURCE_FD_SHM) {
          /* close fd for unexpected fd type from succeeded export */
          if (res_fd_type != VIRGL_RESOURCE_FD_INVALID)
             close(res_fd);
@@ -481,11 +483,24 @@ proxy_context_attach_resource(struct virgl_context *base, struct virgl_resource
          return;
       }
 
-      /* get the actual dma-buf size here because:
+      /* get the actual exported size here because:
        * - pipe resource created by vrend has a zero map_size
        * - blob resource created by vrend can have non-zero fake map_size
        */
-      res_size = lseek(res_fd, 0, SEEK_END);
+      off_t exported_size = lseek(res_fd, 0, SEEK_END);
+      if (exported_size < 0) {
+         /* a shared-memory object need not be seekable, and on an Apple host
+          * it is not; its size is the one the file itself reports. */
+         struct stat st;
+         if (fstat(res_fd, &st) == 0)
+            exported_size = st.st_size;
+      }
+      if (exported_size <= 0) {
+         proxy_log("exported res %d reports no size", res_id);
+         close(res_fd);
+         return;
+      }
+      res_size = (uint64_t)exported_size;
 
       close_res_fd = true;
    }
